#open-question(title: [Does ADHD Add an Impulsivity-Mediated Suicide Channel in ME/CFS?])[
The suicide excess in ADHD (HR 1.89 in the driving meta-analysis; 2.09 in the Taiwanese register) is usually attributed to psychiatric comorbidity, and the Swedish register is consistent with that: adjusting for later-onset comorbidity left a hazard of 1.13 (0.88–1.45), compatible with either no independent effect or a modest effect the study was underpowered to detect for a rare outcome @Corrigan2026ADHDmortality @Chen2019ADHDmortality @Sun2019prematuredeath. An alternative is a partly independent *impulsivity* channel — a shorter interval between ideation and action, more unplanned attempts — which depression screening would not capture.

In ME/CFS the question is open in a specific way. The comorbidity is common enough to matter (20.9% persistent adult ADHD in one CFS cohort of 158 patients, with higher suicide risk in the comorbid group @SaezFrancas2012adhdcfs), and ME/CFS suicide mortality is already elevated several-fold (see @subsec:mortality-summary), but no ME/CFS study has characterised attempt planning or ideation-to-attempt latency by ADHD status. The available evidence cannot distinguish “ADHD adds depression” from “ADHD adds impulsivity on top of depression.”

*Discriminating measurement:* a psychological-autopsy or attempt-characteristics study in ME/CFS comparing unplanned versus planned attempts and ideation-to-action interval in ADHD-comorbid versus depression-only patients, matched for severity. (Severity applicability: unknown.)

*Consequence:* if the impulsivity channel is real, ADHD-specific suicide prevention in ME/CFS would add means restriction and same-day safety planning on top of depression treatment. Means restriction and a written safety plan should already be offered to any ME/CFS patient with suicide risk; the open question only decides whether the ADHD label adds a second, faster-moving risk channel or simply flags a group with more depression.
] <oq:adhd-impulsivity-channel>

#open-question(title: [Is the ADHD-Medication Mortality Association Behavioural or Pharmacological?])[
People with ADHD who take medication show lower all-cause mortality than those who do not, and three further cohorts point the same way @Corrigan2026ADHDmortality @Li2024ADHDpharmmortality @Vasiliadis2024mortality @Rice2024stimulantsuicide; the size, caveats and register overlap of that estimate are set out in @lim:adhd-mortality-transfer-gap. The association is replicated across Swedish, Canadian and US veterans cohorts, and the veterans analysis compared medicated with unmedicated months within the same individuals @Rice2024stimulantsuicide.

The open distinction is mediation. The association could run through the drug itself, through behaviour the drug enables (reduced impulsivity, fewer fatal accidents, better engagement with care), or through both. Two axes matter and are often conflated: *which pathway the effect runs through*, and *whether that pathway operates in ME/CFS*. A pharmacological effect would transfer only if the mortality process it interrupts also drives death in ME/CFS; a behavioural effect would transfer only if the same behaviour change were safe here, which the fatigue-masking concern makes doubtful. Neither question has been tested. No study has asked whether adjusting for impulsivity and functional status attenuates the medication–mortality association.

*Discriminating measurement:* a mediation analysis in an ADHD cohort with medication-adherence, impulsivity and functional-status measures, asking whether the mortality association survives adjustment for the behavioural mediators. (Severity applicability: unknown.)

*Consequence:* the answer decides whether the one modifiable mortality lever in the ADHD literature has any bearing on ME/CFS stimulant prescribing, or whether it is a behavioural effect that cannot be imported — and it is a question about study design, not a treatment option.
] <oq:adhd-medication-mediation>
